diagnosis_text:
CSW1’s OSPF is healthy; there is no CSW1 uplink or OSPF fault to confirm. On CSW1, all OSPF adjacencies are Full on every routed link: bond0 to the core peer (neighbor 10.0.0.78), eth3 to DSW-A1 (10.0.0.79), eth4 to DSW-A2 (10.0.0.80), eth5 to DSW-B1 (10.0.0.81), and eth6 to DSW-B2 (10.0.0.82), per “show ip ospf neighbor/detail.” The CSW1 routing table contains the expected OSPF routes for all office VLANs with ECMP next-hops via those interfaces. frr.log shows no neighbor-down events; only a bond0 speed change and a normal OSPF DD negotiation message.

From the user side, PC1 (10.1.0.100) can reach Office B subnets: successful pings to 10.3.0.100 and 10.5.0.4 confirm east–west reachability. Its default gateway (10.1.0.1) on DSWA is responsive. Therefore, the observed “can’t reach Office B” symptom is not present now and is not explained by any current CSW1 OSPF issue.

Localization: CSW1 OSPF daemon and interfaces bond0, eth3, eth4, eth5, eth6 are all up and exchanging routes. The WAN-facing eth0 is not an OSPF peering link (r1 has no OSPF running), so any “Internet” issues would be upstream of CSW1.

Root cause: No active fault on CSW1; prior alert likely a transient/core-LAG member change or misinterpreted monitoring event.

Corrective fix: None required on CSW1. If intermittent alerts persist, review monitoring thresholds and check LAG member links (eth1/eth2) for flaps; investigate Internet reachability on r1/ISP if that symptom reoccurs.
